WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.697
The Day 19 image has one stage.

2
00:00:04.697 --> 00:00:07.919
So everything used to build the app ships with it.

3
00:00:08.133 --> 00:00:12.511
pip is there, and so is anything a compiler step would leave behind.

4
00:00:12.511 --> 00:00:14.120
And the app runs as root.

5
00:00:14.333 --> 00:00:16.136
None of it helps at run time.

6
00:00:16.136 --> 00:00:24.715
But all of it needs patching, all of it shows up in scan reports, and an attacker who gets into the container can use those tools as well.

7
00:00:25.533 --> 00:00:27.607
A multi-stage build fixes that.

8
00:00:27.607 --> 00:00:33.760
One Dockerfile holds several FROM lines, and each one starts a new stage, from a clean base.

9
00:00:33.967 --> 00:00:36.217
The first stage is called build.

10
00:00:36.217 --> 00:00:42.407
It makes a virtual environment in /venv: one folder that holds a set of Python packages.

11
00:00:42.633 --> 00:00:44.943
It installs the requirements into it.

12
00:00:44.943 --> 00:00:49.687
Then it removes pip from it, because pip is only needed to install packages.

13
00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:54.323
The final stage starts again, from a fresh python:3.14-slim.

14
00:00:54.323 --> 00:00:57.124
Nothing from the build stage is in it.

15
00:00:57.333 --> 00:00:59.941
It removes the base image's own pip too.

16
00:00:59.941 --> 00:01:01.571
The app does not need it.

17
00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:03.205
Now the key line.

18
00:01:03.205 --> 00:01:10.645
COPY --from=build copies one thing across: the /venv folder, with every package inside it.

19
00:01:10.867 --> 00:01:12.550
Then it copies app.py.

20
00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:19.435
And it puts /venv/bin first on the PATH, so the gunicorn in the venv is the one that runs.

21
00:01:19.667 --> 00:01:21.992
Only the last stage becomes the image.

22
00:01:21.992 --> 00:01:26.827
The build stage stays behind, in the build cache, on the machine that built it.

23
00:01:27.533 --> 00:01:28.747
Next, the user.

24
00:01:28.747 --> 00:01:34.331
A container runs as root, user ID 0, unless the image says otherwise.

25
00:01:34.533 --> 00:01:36.473
Namespaces fence the process in.

26
00:01:36.473 --> 00:01:38.958
But it is still root, on a shared kernel.

27
00:01:38.958 --> 00:01:43.321
If an attacker breaks out through a bug, they arrive with root's rights.

28
00:01:43.533 --> 00:01:45.001
Two lines fix it.

29
00:01:45.001 --> 00:01:50.526
useradd creates a normal user, with ID 10001 and no home folder.

30
00:01:50.526 --> 00:01:54.497
USER 10001 makes the running container use it.

31
00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:56.917
Write the number, not the name.

32
00:01:56.917 --> 00:02:04.783
10001 is plainly not zero, so Kubernetes can check that it runs as non-root, without looking inside the image.

33
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.706
The files you copied in still belong to root.

34
00:02:07.706 --> 00:02:11.013
So the app can read its own code, but cannot change it.

35
00:02:11.013 --> 00:02:13.297
Neither can anyone who breaks into it.

36
00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.987
Now check what changed.

37
00:02:15.987 --> 00:02:19.596
The app runs as user 10001, and pip is gone.

38
00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:23.988
For this small Python app, the size barely moves.

39
00:02:23.988 --> 00:02:27.835
The content went from 51.8 megabytes to 49.6.

40
00:02:28.067 --> 00:02:30.081
The bigger gain is in the scan.

41
00:02:30.081 --> 00:02:34.433
Five HIGH findings sat in libraries that pip carries inside itself.

42
00:02:34.433 --> 00:02:36.577
When pip left, they left with it.

43
00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:40.576
For a Kotlin service the difference is large.

44
00:02:40.576 --> 00:02:43.415
The build stage holds a JDK and Gradle.

45
00:02:43.415 --> 00:02:47.347
The final stage needs only a Java runtime and the jar.

46
00:02:48.067 --> 00:02:50.641
Build with the tools in one stage.

47
00:02:50.641 --> 00:02:52.686
Copy across only what runs.

48
00:02:52.686 --> 00:02:55.639
And ship the last stage, as user 10001.
